# (topic, stem, [correct], [wrong], rationale, mnemonic)  SATA if stem ends with (Select all that apply.)
SA=" (Select all that apply.)"
C = [
# --- Assessment & diagnostics
("Assessment","Which history finding in a 2-month-old most suggests a heart problem?",["Sweating and tiring during feedings"],["Spitting up small amounts after feeds","Sleeping about 16 hours a day","Six wet diapers a day"],
 "Feeding is hard work for an infant. Diaphoresis, fatigue, and long feeds signal heart failure. The others are normal infant findings.","\"Feeding is the infant's treadmill.\" Sweaty, tired feeder = check the heart."),
("Assessment","Which tests are used to assess heart disorders in children?"+SA,["Electrocardiogram (ECG)","Chest radiography","Echocardiography","Exercise testing","Cardiac MRI"],["Tympanometry","Lumbar puncture"],
 "The addendum lists ECG, radiography, echocardiography, phonocardiography/MRI, exercise testing, and labs.","\"Every Child's Heart Exam Matters\": ECG, Chest x-ray, Heart echo, Exercise test, MRI."),
("Assessment","Which test best shows a hole between the heart chambers?",["Echocardiogram"],["Electrocardiogram","Pulse oximetry","Phonocardiography"],
 "Echo shows anatomy, valves, and blood flow direction. An ECG shows electrical activity only.","\"Echo = Eyes (sees the anatomy). ECG = Electricity.\""),
("Assessment","A nurse is doing a physical assessment on a child with a suspected heart defect. Which should be included?"+SA,["General appearance and skin color","Pulses in the arms AND legs","Blood pressure","Respiratory rate and effort"],["Tympanic membrane mobility","Visual acuity"],
 "Cardiac physical assessment = general appearance (color, cyanosis, growth), pulses, BP, and respirations.","\"Look, Feel, Measure\": Look (color, appearance), Feel (pulses in all 4 limbs), Measure (BP, HR, RR)."),
# --- VSD
("VSD","Symptoms of a large VSD usually appear at what age?",["4–8 weeks"],["The first hour of life","6 months","School age"],
 "Pulmonary resistance falls over the first weeks, so more blood shunts left → right and the infant becomes symptomatic around 4–8 weeks.","\"4 to 8, the lungs relax and the VSD floods them.\""),
("VSD","Which murmur is classic for a VSD?",["Harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border"],["Continuous machinery murmur","Fixed split S2","Soft musical murmur that is louder lying down"],
 "The addendum says \"fatigue and harsh murmur.\" Machinery = PDA; fixed split = ASD; soft musical = Still's (innocent).","\"VSD = Very loud.\" Small hole, loud howl."),
("VSD","Which statements about a ventricular septal defect are correct?"+SA,["It is an opening between the ventricles","It increases pulmonary blood flow","Many small VSDs close on their own","VSDs that do not close may need surgery"],["It causes cyanosis at birth from right-to-left flow","It always needs emergency prostaglandin E1"],
 "A VSD is a left-to-right (acyanotic) shunt. Most small ones close spontaneously; persistent significant ones need surgical closure.","\"Small hole: wait and watch. Big hole: fix it.\""),
("VSD","A parent asks if their infant's small VSD will need surgery. Which response is best?",["\"Many small VSDs close on their own; the cardiologist will follow it.\""],["\"All VSDs need open-heart surgery right away.\"","\"The loud murmur means it is a large, dangerous hole.\"","\"VSDs never close without surgery.\""],
 "Most small VSDs close spontaneously. A small VSD can be LOUD; loudness does not equal severity.","\"Small hole, loud howl, often closes.\""),
# --- ASD
("ASD","Which heart sound is the classic sign of an atrial septal defect?",["Fixed splitting of the second heart sound (S2)"],["Continuous machinery murmur","Harsh holosystolic murmur","Diastolic murmur at the apex"],
 "Extra right-sided flow delays pulmonic valve closure, so S2 is widely split and doesn't change with breathing.","\"ASD = Always Split, Doesn't change.\""),
("ASD","When is an ASD often discovered?",["Later in childhood, in a child without symptoms"],["At birth because of severe cyanosis","At 4–8 weeks with heart failure","Only after a stroke"],
 "Many children with an ASD have no symptoms; it's found later from a murmur or split S2.","\"ASD = A Silent Defect.\""),
("Increased pulmonary flow","Which defects INCREASE pulmonary blood flow?"+SA,["Ventricular septal defect","Atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","Tricuspid atresia","Pulmonic stenosis"],
 "Left-to-right shunts send extra blood to the lungs. TOF and tricuspid atresia DECREASE pulmonary flow; PS is obstructive.","\"The D's flood the lungs\": ASD, VSD, PDA all end in D."),
# --- PDA
("PDA","The ductus arteriosus connects which two structures?",["Pulmonary artery and aorta"],["Right and left atria","Right and left ventricles","Umbilical vein and inferior vena cava"],
 "In the fetus it lets blood skip the lungs. It should start closing with the first breaths (the addendum says closure is complete by about 3 months).","\"Ductus ARTERIOSUS connects the two big ARTERIES.\""),
("PDA","Which findings are expected with a significant PDA?"+SA,["Continuous machinery murmur","Wide pulse pressure","Bounding pulses"],["Weak femoral pulses with high arm blood pressure","Fixed split S2","Cyanosis at birth"],
 "Blood runs aorta → pulmonary artery during systole AND diastole, which makes the continuous murmur and wide pulse pressure.","\"PDA = Machinery, Wide, Bounding\" (\"Mighty Wide Bounce\")."),
("PDA","A premature infant has a symptomatic PDA. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?",["Indomethacin"],["Prostaglandin E1","Digoxin","Furosemide only"],
 "Indomethacin (or ibuprofen) blocks prostaglandin and helps the ductus close in preemies.","\"Indomethacin Interrupts (closes) it. Prostaglandin keeps it Patent (open).\""),
("PDA","Which are management options for a PDA?"+SA,["Indomethacin in selected premature infants","Closure during cardiac catheterization","Surgical ligation"],["Prostaglandin E1 to close it","Antibiotics to dissolve it"],
 "The addendum lists indomethacin, cardiac catheterization, and surgery. PGE1 does the OPPOSITE: it keeps the ductus open.","\"Pill, Plug, or Procedure\": Indomethacin (pill), cath device (plug), surgery (procedure)."),
# --- Pulmonic stenosis
("Pulmonic stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis causes hypertrophy of which chamber?",["Right ventricle"],["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium only"],
 "The right ventricle must push blood through a narrow pulmonary valve, so it thickens.","\"Pulmonic = Pushes the Right side.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","Which findings are associated with pulmonic stenosis?"+SA,["Systolic ejection murmur","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Signs of right-sided heart failure","Cyanosis when severe"],["Continuous machinery murmur","Bounding pulses"],
 "The addendum: mild right-sided heart failure, cyanosis, systolic ejection murmur (SEM). Critical PS can limit lung blood flow, causing cyanosis.","\"PS = Pressure on the Right, Systolic murmur.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","What is the usual treatment to relieve pulmonic stenosis?",["Balloon angioplasty/valvuloplasty"],["Indomethacin","Lifelong prostaglandin E1","Bed rest only"],
 "A balloon catheter stretches the narrow valve open.","\"Narrow valve? Balloon it open.\""),
# --- Aortic stenosis
("Aortic stenosis","Aortic stenosis leads to hypertrophy of which chamber?",["Left ventricle"],["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium only"],
 "The left ventricle must push blood through a narrow aortic valve.","\"Aortic = Left (A comes Last, on the Left side).\" Pulmonic = Right."),
("Aortic stenosis","Which are concerns in a child with aortic stenosis?"+SA,["Chest pain with exertion","Fainting (syncope)","Risk of sudden death","A heart murmur"],["Continuous machinery murmur","High arm BP with weak femoral pulses"],
 "The addendum: usually no symptoms except a murmur, but chest pain and even sudden death can occur. Exertional symptoms are red flags.","\"AS = Avoid Sudden exertion\": chest pain, syncope, sudden death."),
("Aortic stenosis","A teen with known aortic stenosis wants to join the basketball team. Which response is best?",["\"You need a cardiology evaluation before being cleared for sports.\""],["\"Sports are fine since you have no symptoms.\"","\"Just drink extra water during games.\"","\"Only avoid sports if you get a fever.\""],
 "Exertion can cause chest pain, syncope, or sudden death in AS. Sports clearance must come from cardiology.","\"Faints while running = Referral.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","Which are treatment options for aortic stenosis?"+SA,["Beta blocker or calcium channel blocker for stabilization","Balloon valvuloplasty","Valve replacement"],["Indomethacin","Prostaglandin E1 for long-term treatment"],
 "The addendum: stabilization with a beta blocker or CCB, balloon valvuloplasty, or valve replacement.","\"Calm it, Balloon it, or Replace it.\""),
# --- Coarctation
("Coarctation","Which finding is classic for coarctation of the aorta?",["Higher blood pressure in the arms than the legs"],["Higher blood pressure in the legs than the arms","Continuous machinery murmur","Fixed split S2"],
 "The narrowing blocks flow to the lower body: pressure is high above it and low below it, with weak or absent femoral pulses.","\"Strong upstairs, weak downstairs.\""),
("Coarctation","Which symptoms may an older child with coarctation of the aorta have?"+SA,["Headache","Dizziness (vertigo)","Nosebleeds","Leg pain with activity"],["Bounding femoral pulses","Cyanosis of the lips at rest"],
 "High pressure in the upper body causes headache, vertigo, and nosebleeds (even stroke); poor flow to the legs causes leg pain.","\"Head, Nose, Legs\": high pressure up top (headache, nosebleed), low flow down below (leg pain)."),
("Coarctation","What is the best screening action for coarctation?",["Compare pulses and BP in the arms and legs"],["Check oxygen saturation only","Listen for a fixed split S2","Check capillary refill in one hand"],
 "Four-extremity pulse and BP comparison catches the arm-leg difference.","\"Check all 4 corners\": both arms, both legs."),
("Coarctation","A 3-day-old with coarctation suddenly becomes pale, poorly perfused, and in shock. What is the most likely cause?",["The ductus arteriosus is closing"],["The foramen ovale is opening","Normal newborn transition","Dehydration from breastfeeding"],
 "In severe neonatal CoA, the open ductus was supplying the lower body. When it closes, perfusion drops. PGE1 can reopen it.","\"Duct closes → baby crashes.\""),
("Coarctation","Which serious complication can result from uncorrected coarctation of the aorta?",["Stroke (CVA)"],["Coronary artery aneurysm","Mitral valve prolapse","Hearing loss"],
 "Chronic high blood pressure in the upper body can cause a CVA (listed on the addendum).","\"High pressure upstairs → head trouble\": headache, nosebleed, stroke."),
# --- Tricuspid atresia
("Tricuspid atresia","In tricuspid atresia, how does blood leave the right atrium?",["Through the foramen ovale into the left atrium"],["Through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle","Through the ductus venosus","Through the mitral valve into the right ventricle"],
 "The tricuspid valve is closed, so blood must cross the foramen ovale, then reach the lungs through a PDA (or VSD).","\"Front door locked? Take the side door\" (the foramen ovale)."),
("Tricuspid atresia","An infant with tricuspid atresia is at risk when the fetal shunts close. Which signs would the nurse expect?"+SA,["Cyanosis","Tachycardia","Dyspnea"],["Wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses","Fixed split S2"],
 "The addendum: when these structures close, cyanosis, tachycardia, and dyspnea occur.","\"Shunts shut → Blue, Breathless, and Beating fast.\""),
("Tricuspid atresia","An infant with tricuspid atresia is receiving an IV prostaglandin E1 infusion. What is the nursing priority?",["Monitor for apnea"],["Monitor for hypertension","Restrict all oral fluids","Check for hearing loss"],
 "PGE1 keeps the ductus open until surgery, but it can cause apnea. Continuous monitoring is required.","\"PGE1 = Patent ductus, Pause in breathing.\""),
# --- TOF
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which are the four defects in Tetralogy of Fallot?"+SA,["Pulmonary stenosis","Ventricular septal defect","Overriding (dextroposed) aorta","Right ventricular hypertrophy"],["Atrial septal defect","Coarctation of the aorta"],
 "The addendum: PS, VSD, dextroposition of the aorta, and RV hypertrophy.","\"PROV\": Pulmonary stenosis, RVH, Overriding aorta, VSD."),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","A child with TOF has polycythemia. The nurse understands this is:",["The body making more red blood cells because of low oxygen"],["A sign of iron deficiency","Caused by too much oxygen","An infection"],
 "Chronic hypoxemia stimulates RBC production to carry more oxygen.","\"Low O2 → More Reds.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which findings reflect chronic hypoxemia in uncorrected TOF?"+SA,["Cyanosis","Clubbing of the fingers","Polycythemia","Growth restriction","Dyspnea"],["Bounding pulses","High arm BP with weak femoral pulses"],
 "The addendum's TOF signs: cyanosis, polycythemia, dyspnea, growth restriction, clubbing.","\"Blue, Clubbed, Thick blood, Small, and Short of breath.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","An infant with TOF suddenly becomes very blue and breathes fast while crying. What should the nurse do FIRST?",["Place the infant in a knee-chest position"],["Lay the infant flat on the back","Feed the infant to calm them","Give albuterol"],
 "Knee-chest raises systemic resistance, which pushes more blood into the lungs. Then calm the infant, give O2, and get emergency help.","\"Tet spell? Tuck like a frog.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Why does a child with uncorrected TOF squat after playing?",["Squatting increases systemic resistance and sends more blood to the lungs"],["Squatting rests tired leg muscles","Squatting lowers blood pressure","Squatting closes the VSD"],
 "Squatting kinks the leg arteries, raising systemic resistance so less blood crosses the VSD and more goes to the lungs.","\"Squat = Squeeze the legs, Send blood to the lungs.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","A child with TOF and polycythemia has vomiting and diarrhea. Why is this a concern?",["Dehydration thickens the blood and raises the risk of clots and stroke"],["It lowers the red blood cell count","It causes the VSD to close","It is not a concern"],
 "Polycythemic blood is already thick. Dehydration makes it thicker, raising the risk of thrombosis or stroke. Keep the child hydrated.","\"Thick blood + dry = clot risk\": keep them hydrated."),
# --- CHF
("Heart failure","Which are signs of RIGHT-sided heart failure?"+SA,["Hepatomegaly","Increased venous pressure","Edema/weight gain from fluid"],["Crackles in the lungs","Dyspnea as the first sign"],
 "Right-sided failure backs fluid up into the body (liver, veins). Left-sided failure backs up into the LUNGS (crackles, dyspnea).","\"Left = Lungs. Right = the Rest of the body\" (liver)."),
("Heart failure","Which finding indicates LEFT-sided heart failure?",["Crackles (rales) in the lungs"],["Hepatomegaly","Jugular venous distention","Ascites"],
 "Left-sided failure → fluid backs up into the lungs: dyspnea, crackles, cyanosis. It eventually causes right-sided failure too.","\"Left = Lungs.\""),
("Heart failure","Which interventions reduce the heart's workload in a child with heart failure?"+SA,["Diuretics","Inotropic medications such as digoxin","Vasodilators","Small, frequent feedings with rest periods"],["Increasing fluid intake","Large feedings every 6 hours"],
 "The addendum: reduce workload with diuretics, inotropes, and vasodilators. Small frequent feeds save energy.","\"Dump, Pump, Relax\": Diuretics dump fluid, Inotropes help it pump, Vasodilators relax the vessels."),
("Heart failure","What are the EARLIEST signs of heart failure in an infant?",["Tachycardia and tachypnea"],["Bradycardia and hypertension","Ankle edema","Clubbing"],
 "The addendum lists tachycardia and tachypnea first. Infants show HF through fast breathing, fast heart rate, and feeding trouble before edema.","\"Fast and Fast\": fast heart, fast breathing."),
("Heart failure","Before giving digoxin to an infant, what should the nurse do?",["Count the apical pulse for 1 full minute and hold if below the prescribed limit"],["Take the radial pulse for 15 seconds","Give it with a full bottle of formula","Check the blood pressure only"],
 "Digoxin slows the heart. An apical pulse for a full minute is required; hold and notify if below parameters (often under 90–110 in infants).","\"Dig: count a full minute, then Don't go if it's low.\""),
("Heart failure","A child receives furosemide and digoxin. Which lab value is the priority to monitor?",["Potassium"],["Calcium","Glucose","Hemoglobin A1c"],
 "Furosemide wastes potassium, and low potassium increases the risk of digoxin toxicity.","\"Lasix Loses K → Dig gets Dangerous.\""),
# --- Rheumatic fever
("Rheumatic fever","Which findings are consistent with rheumatic fever?"+SA,["Chorea","New systolic murmur","Swollen, tender joints","Macular rash on the trunk (erythema marginatum)","Subcutaneous nodules"],["Strawberry tongue","Peeling of the fingertips"],
 "These are the Jones major criteria from the addendum. Strawberry tongue and peeling point to Kawasaki.","\"JONES\": Joints, O (♥ carditis), Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."),
("Rheumatic fever","Which lab result shows a recent strep infection in suspected rheumatic fever?",["Elevated ASO titer"],["Positive blood culture for Staph","Low platelet count","Elevated troponin only"],
 "The addendum: positive ASO titer plus increased ESR and CRP. ASO shows PRIOR strep; it doesn't diagnose RF by itself.","\"ASO = Already Strep Occurred.\""),
("Rheumatic fever","The nurse notes chorea in a child with rheumatic fever. Which describes chorea?",["Sudden, involuntary, purposeless movements of the limbs"],["A painless rash on the trunk","Swelling of the large joints","Firm nodules over tendons"],
 "Sydenham chorea = jerky involuntary movements. It may appear later than other signs.","\"Chorea = Choreography gone wild\" (dancing that the child can't control)."),
("Rheumatic fever","What determines the long-term prognosis of rheumatic fever?",["How much the heart (especially the valves) is involved"],["How high the fever got","How many joints were swollen","Whether the rash was itchy"],
 "Arthritis heals without damage; carditis can leave permanent valve disease (mitral/aortic).","\"Joints heal, the Heart remembers.\" Rheumatic = Valves."),
("Rheumatic fever","Which are part of managing acute rheumatic fever?"+SA,["Antibiotics to eliminate group A strep","Bed rest during the acute phase","Ongoing prophylaxis to prevent recurrence"],["Ear tubes","IVIG as first-line therapy"],
 "The addendum: bed rest and antibiotics to eliminate GABHS. Secondary prophylaxis (e.g., benzathine penicillin) prevents recurrence.","\"Kill the strep, Rest the heart, Prevent the repeat.\""),
# --- Kawasaki
("Kawasaki","Which are EARLY signs of Kawasaki disease?"+SA,["High fever that doesn't respond to antipyretics","Swollen hands and feet","Strawberry tongue and red, cracked lips","Red eyes (conjunctival injection) without discharge","Enlarged cervical lymph nodes"],["Peeling skin on the fingers","Coronary artery aneurysm"],
 "Peeling (desquamation) and aneurysms are LATE findings on the addendum.","\"CRASH and BURN\": Conjunctivitis, Rash, Adenopathy, Strawberry tongue, Hands/feet + BURN (fever for 5+ days)."),
("Kawasaki","Which are LATE findings in Kawasaki disease?"+SA,["Peeling skin (desquamation)","Rising platelet count","Coronary artery aneurysm"],["Onset of high fever","Strawberry tongue appearing for the first time"],
 "The addendum's late signs: desquamation, increased platelets, aneurysms. High platelets raise the clot risk in aneurysms.","\"Late = Peel, Platelets, Pouches\" (aneurysms are pouches)."),
("Kawasaki","What is the main purpose of IV immunoglobulin (IVIG) in Kawasaki disease?",["To decrease the immune response and lower the risk of coronary aneurysms"],["To kill the bacteria causing the disease","To close the ductus arteriosus","To raise the platelet count"],
 "IVIG calms the inflammation; given early (within about 10 days) it reduces coronary damage. NOTE: your addendum lists ibuprofen for inflammation, but your integrated study guide and current AHA guidance use ASPIRIN with IVIG. Ibuprofen can block aspirin's anti-clotting effect. Ask your professor which answer they expect.","\"Kawasaki Crowns the Coronaries.\" IVIG protects the crowns."),
("Kawasaki","Which follow-up is MOST important after Kawasaki disease?",["Serial echocardiograms to check the coronary arteries"],["Yearly hearing tests","Monthly throat cultures","Daily blood glucose checks"],
 "The big risk is coronary aneurysm, which echo detects. Most children recover fully, but some need heart surgery.","\"Echo the coronaries.\""),
("Kawasaki","A child received IVIG for Kawasaki disease. When can the MMR and varicella vaccines be given?",["11 months after IVIG"],["Right away","1 month after IVIG","Never"],
 "IVIG's antibodies block the response to live vaccines. Non-live vaccines don't need to wait.","\"2 grams → wait 11 months.\""),
]
